  "id": "Compound::DB01101 - Disease::DOID:10534",
  "verbalization": "Capecitabine, a fluoropyrimidine chemotherapy agent, is implicated in the development of Stomach Cancer through a chain of causal relationships involving Docetaxel. The evidence suggests that Capecitabine may cause conditions such as Hand And Foot Syndrome Secondary To Sickle Cell Anaemia, Necrotizing Enterocolitis, and Necrotising Enterocolitis Neonatal, all of which are linked to Docetaxel. Docetaxel, in turn, is used to treat Stomach Cancer, indicating a potential indirect pathway where Capecitabine's effects may contribute to the progression or manifestation of Stomach Cancer through the activation of Docetaxel. The shared lowest common ancestor of Capecitabine and Docetaxel, both belonging to the Carbonyl Compound class, suggests a biochemical or pharmacological link that may underpin this relationship. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Docetaxel as a therapeutic agent for Stomach Cancer, while Capecitabine's involvement in the pathogenesis of related conditions may offer a mechanistic basis for its potential role in the development of Stomach Cancer.",
